You are given a sorted array of integers [a1, a2, ..., an]. We can perform any number of operations of the following type on this array: mark the first element of the array, as well as all elements that are not equal to their left neighbors (that is, all elements i such that ai != ai-1), then either delete all marked elements or duplicate them (that is, replace each marked element with two identical elements).1
For example, consider the array [1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 4, 5] (marked elements are shown in bold). If we delete all marked elements, we get [1, 1, 4], and if we duplicate them, we get [1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5].
The operations cannot be performed if the array becomes empty. After each operation, every element gets unmarked.
We call an array of integers b reachable if it can be obtained from array a by some number of the described operations. Your task is to count the number of reachable arrays of length k.

Input:
The first line contains one integer t (1 <= t <= 10^4) — the number of test cases.
Each test case consists of two lines: the first line contains two integers n and k (1 <= n, k <= 3 * 10^5); the second line contains n integers a1, a2, ..., an (1 <= a1 <= a2 <= ... <= an <= n). Note that the array is sorted.
Additional constraint on the input: the sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 3 * 10^5.

Output:
For each test case, output one integer — the number of reachable arrays b of length k. It can be shown that, under the constraints of the problem, the answer fits into a standard 32-bit integer type.

Strategy:
The idea is that each distinct number has a count. There can be multiple numbers with the same counts which links them together since the rules dictate that we can basically add 1 duplicate to any existing number or decrease the count of all number by 1. It doesn't really matter what the numbers are, only their counts. Therefore we can compress the state by putting them in a hashmap tracking the count of each. We can compress that further by tracking the numbers of numbers with a certain count. Then we can move these into a vector and sort them since the rule is that for each group of a certain size all the groups of bigger size must be present too and all smaller groups might or might not be present. This is the key to finding the numbers of possible arrays. We loop from biggest to smallest size and track 3 things. The count of possibilities, the distinct numbers we went through so far (this is saved in the compressed state at the second position of a pair, first is the size of the group) and the sum of all their counts. (if we have 3 groups of sizes {1,4,5} and the counts of elements in these groups is {2,3,4} we have 34 distinct elements in total which is the difference in size we are going to get when performing an operation. Inside the loop, we are basically asking if an array using these elements from our compressed state is possible (since doing delete and add are reverses of eachother if the delete did not delete the last number in its group the number or arrays is not large). We track the size of each element of this group and the number of elements in the group. The distinct counts get updated by increasing it with the number of elements, the sum gets increasing by cnt_elements*size_group since thats the total number of elements that are new. Now we compute the delta needed to get to k. This is k-sum. Then we do a simple check delta%distinct_elements==0. If this is true then it could be possible to reach an array of size k with the groups of size current and larger. We proceed by calculating delta/distinct. This represents the number of deletions or additions needed to reach size k. We do this because if abs(delta/distinct) is bigger than the size of the smallest group (current) we will delete an element completely and break the uniformness of the math and since we are trying to compute if we can reach k by using the ALL the distinct elements in the current group and larger we stop if this happens and move on. If abs(delta/distinct) is smaller than current, we increase the answer count. We repeat this for all the group sizes going down outputting the final ans count.